      "start_line": 16087,
      "end_line": 16101,
      "text": "\\]\nFiniteness follows from $u^2=z$, and $u=x/(zy^2)$ gives\nbirationality. The image ring contains all even powers of $u$\nand the odd monomials $u^iv^j$ with $i\\geq3,j\\geq2$.\nSince the normalization is generated over the image ring by $1,u$,\nits conductor consists of the series $h$ for which both $h$ and $uh$\nlie in that ring. The even--odd monomial description therefore gives\n$u^2v^2\\mathbb C\\{u,v\\}$, with divisor\n$2\\{u=0\\}+2\\{v=0\\}$. Its differential minors are\n$3u^2v^2,-4u^4v,-2u$, and generate the ideal $(u)$. Thus the rank-drop locus is exactly\nthe divisor $\\{u=0\\}$, which maps by $v\\mapsto(0,v,0)$\nto a line. The finite rank-drop hypothesis of\nProposition~\\ref{night:P4.15} fails for this morphism.\nThe exponent-matrix, transfer-pairing and normalization computations\nare listed in Table~\\ref{tab:II:programs}.\n",
      "sha256": "1bafaf830c36e3fb737b84b643d7cc8a6d91398c622614a62e0ec65cb4205aad"
    }
  ],
